<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.1" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="42349-1" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Reason for referral (narrative)"/>
    <title>Reason for Referral</title>
    <text><paragraph>Suspected inflammatory bowel disease in a woman of 21 with four months of right lower abdominal pain, diarrhea, fevers, and weight loss, with iron deficiency anemia and a fecal calprotectin of 1240 ug/g. Ileocolonoscopy, MR enterography, and tuberculosis screening are being arranged. Please see her once they are done to confirm the diagnosis and plan treatment with her. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Urgency: Urgent, within two weeks. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Questions for the consultant:</paragraph><list listType="ordered"><item>Do the endoscopic, histologic, and imaging findings confirm Crohn disease, and is intestinal tuberculosis adequately excluded?</item><item>How would you classify her disease and its risk of a disabling course?</item><item>Which advanced therapy do you recommend, and should a corticosteroid bridge be used?</item><item>What screening and vaccines are needed before immunosuppression?</item><item>How should her anemia, nutrition, and bone health be managed?</item><item>What are the treatment targets and the monitoring plan, and what should she know about fertility and pregnancy?</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Ms. Boulanger is seen at the request of Dr. Marchetti-Rowe with four months of right lower abdominal cramping, five or six loose stools a day with one at night, evening fevers, fatigue, and loss of 6 kg. The ileocolonoscopy five days ago showed deep longitudinal ulcers over 15 cm of the terminal ileum and patchy ulcers in the ascending and transverse colon with a spared rectum (SES-CD 14); MR enterography showed 18 cm of inflamed terminal ileum without stricture, fistula, or abscess. She has no perianal, joint, eye, or skin symptoms. She has never smoked. She was told directly that the findings show Crohn disease, a lifelong condition that can be controlled, and asked to hear the treatment choices with their trade-offs. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>Lili Boulanger was born Marie-Juliette Olga Boulanger on August 21, 1893, in the 9th arrondissement of Paris. [D]</item><item>She was a composer, the sister of the composer and teacher Nadia Boulanger (1887–1979). [D]</item><item>At about two years old, in 1895, she had pneumonia, described as bronchial pneumonia; her health was frail from a very young age and she remained sickly for the rest of her life. [D]</item><item>The usual account is that the childhood pneumonia left her immune system weakened, so that she never fully recovered. [R]</item><item>In 1913, at 19, she became the first woman to win the Premier Grand Prix de Rome for musical composition, with the cantata Faust et Hélène; she had been the only woman among the five finalists that May. [D]</item><item>She went to the Villa Medici in Rome in 1914. The outbreak of war made her leave Rome for Nice, where she composed the song cycle Clairières dans le ciel; she stayed in Rome again in 1916 and then returned to France. [D]</item><item>Early in 1915, living in Paris, she began to have headaches, loss of appetite, and fever, followed by symptoms resembling colitis and appendicitis; she spent much of her time in bed but kept composing. [D]</item></list><paragraph>5 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

