<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.8"/>
    <code code="51848-0" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation note"/>
    <title>Assessment</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item>Crohn disease of the terminal ileum and colon, Montreal A2 L3 B1, moderate to severe (K50.80). Long ileal ulcers, skip lesions, a spared rectum, small non-caseating granulomas, and the MR findings confirm Crohn disease. Intestinal tuberculosis can look the same; against it here are a negative interferon-gamma release assay, a normal chest X-ray, no caseating or confluent granulomas, no necrotic nodes, and negative acid-fast stain and TB PCR. That is enough to start treatment, but the mycobacterial culture must be checked when final. Diagnosis before 30, extensive deep ulcers, and weight loss predict a disabling course, so she should start an advanced therapy now. [S]</item><item>Choice of therapy. Options discussed with her: infliximab with azathioprine, adalimumab, ustekinumab, risankizumab, and vedolizumab. She chose risankizumab: effective for induction and maintenance in ileocolonic disease, given as three infusions and then a home injection every eight weeks, without an added immunomodulator and with a low rate of serious infection. The trade-off she accepted is that data in pregnancy are more limited than for anti-TNF drugs. A short prednisone bridge will control symptoms until risankizumab acts. [S]</item><item>Screening before immunosuppression. Tuberculosis screen negative. Hepatitis B surface antigen and core antibody negative, but surface antibody below 10 after an infant series: a booster is advised. Immune to measles and varicella, so no live vaccine is needed and treatment need not wait. [S]</item><item>Anemia, nutrition, and bone. Iron deficiency from blood loss and inflammation is best treated intravenously. Moderate malnutrition needs dietitian support. Vitamin D deficiency, low weight, and a steroid course call for vitamin D, calcium during the steroid, and a baseline DXA. [S]</item><item>Targets, fertility, and pregnancy. The targets are clinical response early, steroid-free remission and a normal CRP by about week 12, a normal fecal calprotectin by about six months, and endoscopic healing at six to twelve months. Crohn disease in remission does not reduce fertility; the main risk to a pregnancy is active disease, so a pregnancy should be planned in remission. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Data reviewed: Ileocolonoscopy images and report; biopsy histology with acid-fast stain and TB PCR (mycobacterial culture pending); MR enterography; chest X-ray; interferon-gamma release assay; hepatitis B, hepatitis C, HIV, measles, and varicella serology; stool tests and celiac serology; blood counts, iron studies, CRP, albumin, vitamin D, and fecal calprotectin; Dr. Marchetti-Rowe&apos;s referral. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.10" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="18776-5" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Plan of care note"/>
    <title>Plan of Treatment</title>
    <text><list><item>Risankizumab 600 mg intravenously at weeks 0, 4, and 8, then 360 mg subcutaneously every 8 weeks from week 12, with liver enzymes and bilirubin at baseline and through at least week 12. [S]</item><item>Prednisone 40 mg daily, tapered by 5 mg each week to stop at eight weeks; no further steroid courses without a review of the plan. [S]</item><item>Review the final mycobacterial culture before the third infusion. [S]</item><item>Before the first infusion: PCV20, recombinant zoster vaccine dose 1, and a hepatitis B booster, with anti-HBs checked one to two months later; zoster dose 2 two to six months later; inactivated influenza and COVID-19 vaccines each season; no live vaccines. [S]</item><item>Ferric derisomaltose 1000 mg intravenously with the first infusion; vitamin D3 2000 units daily; calcium while on prednisone; dietitian support with oral supplements; baseline DXA. [S]</item><item>Treat to target: symptoms and CRP at each visit, fecal calprotectin at about 12 weeks and six months, and ileocolonoscopy at six to twelve months. [S]</item><item>Contraception while the disease is active; plan any pregnancy after three to six months of steroid-free remission, with a preconception review and folic acid. [S]</item><item>IBD psychology if her mood or anxiety does not improve, and practical support so she can rehearse and perform. [S]</item><item>Discussed with Dr. Marchetti-Rowe the same day; Ms. Boulanger has a written summary of her diagnosis, her treatment choice, and the monitoring plan, and agreed to it. [S]</item><item>Follow-up: IBD clinic at the week-4 infusion and at week 12; IBD nurse line between visits. Primary care coordinates vaccines, nutrition, and mood screening. [S]</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>
